From 8a58c8eeed107d3fea833d829c41ed9943c2063d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: milliluk-erik <build@localhost>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:40:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/49] emuext: guest->host LOG side-channel + named

Add the lwasm emulator-extension opcodes LOG ($103E) and BREAK ($113E) as a
guest->host side channel: the 6809 core decodes them and routes them to the
machine through four debug_cpu delegates.

THE DELEGATES ARE OPTIONAL, AND THE CORE MUST BEHAVE WHEN THEY ARE ABSENT.
The core decodes these opcodes unconditionally, for every machine it drives,
whether or not -emuext was passed -- that is deliberate, so the behaviour is
well defined rather than depending on a command-line flag.  But it means the
decode path is reachable on a machine that implements none of this, by a wild
PC walking over ordinary data.  (Not hypothetical: see patch 35.)  So the two
action delegates go through DELEGATE_SAFE_CALL, state_read is .func-checked at
its use, and debug_read -- which is called FIRST, to gather the text, before
any of those -- is checked by mc6809_emuext_supported() before the handler
does anything at all.  A machine that wired nothing takes its native
illegal-opcode behaviour, which is what the hardware it models does.  Wiring
debug_read is what opts a machine in.

Implement it for both 6809 machine families.  coco3.c answers ${xpc}, ${paddr},
${block} and ${task} from the GIME's MMU; dragon/dragon.c covers every
Dragon-family machine -- Dragon 32/64, Dragon Professional, CoCo 1/2, Deluxe
CoCo -- and answers only what a machine with no MMU can honestly answer,
returning -1 for the rest so the LOG handler echoes ${paddr} back untouched
instead of substituting a plausible-looking wrong number.  Not the MC-10:
that is an MC6803 and never reaches this code.

BREAK, LOG output and the side-effect-free read are identical work on any
machine, so they are written once as emuext_break()/emuext_log()/emuext_read()
in debug.c and the machines supply only state_read.  A second copy of the LOG
output path in particular is a standing trap: it is the copy that gets fixed
in one file and forgotten in the other.
